Z||000000||WisconsinGT||03-19-2000||08:00 PM||||to reset the computer on are Fiero's? I am talking about when you unhook a cable from the battery and hook it back onto the terminal. Do you try to find a flat road and drive 55 for several miles? Or is it 35-45 miles per hour? Does it matter?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||03-19-2000||10:08 PM||||I don't think it really matters. I have had the ECM without power ranging from a few hours, to almost three weeks. It has never had to recalibrate. If you replace a sensor like the IAC, seating of the pintle will require "the drive" but not because you just disconnected the system.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-19-2000||10:25 PM||||Retraining the ECM isn't a 2 minute thing.<P>When you reset the ECM it loses all the learned data as well as needing to home the IAC. The IAC will zero once the car is over about 35mph for about a minute. ((may depend on exactly what ECM. DIS ECM seems to zero the IAC anytime the engine is over 22-2500 RPM for more than about 30sec. Even if you aren't driving.)) As far as I can tell the IAC is zeroed every time the car is run wether it's really needed or not. If you haven't worked on the IAC then you probably won't notice much of an idle problem after an ECM reset.<P>The learned data will accumulate and fine tune over the next few days. This data is constantly adjusted whenever the car runs. When the ECM is first reset there is no learned data, and the ECM runs entirely from the PROM. Untill the ECM accumulates all the learned data it needs you can get some fairly squirly things going on. Don't assume there is a real problem unless a performance problems last more than a day or 2.<P>Also changing brands or grades of gas can throw off the learned data. It can take a few miles for the learned table to settle to the new fuel.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-19-2000).]||||reg||1
